\section*{Sharp logarithmic exponents for fixed off-diagonal Ramsey numbers}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Ramsey logarithms for clique size at least six}: For every fixed $s\ge6$, the manuscript claims $r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$; the separate parameter $s=5$ is treated by another manuscript.

\textit{The logarithmic exponent of r(5,t)}: The companion manuscript claims $r(5,t)=t^4/(\log t)^{3+o(1)}$; the lower estimate allows any arbitrarily small extra logarithmic power, while the matching upper estimate has power three.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Ramsey logarithms for clique size at least six}: Random incident point--hyperplane flags define an ordered graph in projective dimension $s-1$. Linear independence forbids the target clique, while independent sequences satisfy an asymmetric consistency rule. Sparse-incidence descriptions, validation samples, and dimensional projection give short encodings of such sequences, contradicting the entropy needed for a long independent set.

\textit{The logarithmic exponent of r(5,t)}: Random flags in projective four-space produce a graph without five-cliques. Two marking scans and a small set of representative flags bound the information in a purported long consistent sequence. Geometric incidence analysis controls exceptional subspace rectangles, causing its upper entropy bound to conflict with the entropy forced by occurrence in the original random stream.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Ramsey logarithms for clique size at least six}: The primary theorem covers every fixed s>=6 and both bounds, but not s=5. Together the two primary manuscripts cover the s>=5 question.

\textit{The logarithmic exponent of r(5,t)}: The primary theorem covers only s=5, with the sharp log exponent three and both bounds; it does not cover s>=6. Together the two primary manuscripts cover the s>=5 question.

This page records partial support. Complete resolution of the catalogue question is not established by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 170 describes Lean coverage for the Ramsey logarithmic exponents at clique size five and at every fixed clique size at least six.

\textit{Ramsey logarithms for clique size at least six}: The manuscript links \texttt{RamseyFive.lean}, \texttt{SharpLogRamsey.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

\textit{The logarithmic exponent of r(5,t)}: The manuscript links \texttt{RamseyFive.lean}, \texttt{SharpLogRamsey.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

